WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:06.021
Today you are in for a treat, because&nbsp;
we are going to perform brain to audio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.021 --> 00:00:12.381
yes listen to music straight from&nbsp;
your brain, kind of, and more. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.381 --> 00:00:15.921
I hear you asking, Doctor,&nbsp;
how is this even possible?

00:00:15.921 --> 00:00:22.221
Well, this new paper showcases an EEG-based&nbsp;
technique to perform this science-fiction like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:29.421
experiment. Normally, EEG, electroencephalography&nbsp;
means that we place electrodes on the scalp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.421 --> 00:00:35.961
and then, we listen to the brain from the&nbsp;
outside. And by that, I mean looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.961 --> 00:00:43.641
the electric activity. However, here, this work&nbsp;
uses Intracranial EEG, that requires electrodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:50.061
to be on the surface of the brain. Thus making&nbsp;
it an invasive technique that makes scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.061 --> 00:00:56.061
able to listen to the brain straight from&nbsp;
its surface. So what can we do with that?

00:00:56.061 --> 00:01:03.081
And the answer is: absolutely miraculous things.&nbsp;
Let’s look at the experiment design and see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.081 --> 00:01:09.681
happened. First, a song is played to 29 patients,&nbsp;
this was Another Brick in the Wall the classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.681 --> 00:01:17.001
from Pink Floyd, then an auditory spectrogram is&nbsp;
created from this song. This enables us to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.001 --> 00:01:24.081
at the song and understand a little better where&nbsp;
we are and what is happening. Then, scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.081 --> 00:01:30.141
recorded the neural activity of the brain at&nbsp;
the points where the electrodes were placed, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.141 --> 00:01:37.341
created a spectrogram from information that was&nbsp;
obtained from the brain. This is a decoding step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.341 --> 00:01:45.501
and the one in this particular diagram was done by&nbsp;
a neural network. Decoding real neural information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:53.781
by using a digital neural network. What a time to&nbsp;
be alive! Now, hold on to your papers, because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.781 --> 00:02:01.341
are going to look at the two spectrograms, and…oh&nbsp;
my goodness. The information that was read from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:07.821
the brain indeed refers to the same song. No&nbsp;
question about it. Due to copyright constraints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.821 --> 00:02:12.561
I can’t play it here, but you can listen to&nbsp;
it through the link in the video description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.561 --> 00:02:19.461
and I have to say that of course, this is not&nbsp;
HiFi quality, but if you know the song, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.461 --> 00:02:26.061
tell what is being played in the brain, and even&nbsp;
sing the lyrics to it. Absolutely incredible.

00:02:26.061 --> 00:02:30.801
The paper also showcases a ton of&nbsp;
little golden nuggets of information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:38.421
I loved this chart that tells us exactly how&nbsp;
the quality of the reconstruction increases with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.421 --> 00:02:43.941
the number of electrodes, little measurement&nbsp;
points being used on the brain. Adding more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.941 --> 00:02:47.961
of typically typically increases the&nbsp;
resolution of the information we get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.961 --> 00:02:56.001
but also increases the noise as well. So here,&nbsp;
a proper way of decoding helps a great deal.

00:02:56.001 --> 00:03:01.821
This paper is an interesting showcase of what&nbsp;
is possible through the power of research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.821 --> 00:03:07.281
and since this data has to be read from the&nbsp;
surface of your brain, I would not worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.281 --> 00:03:12.621
about anyone eavesdropping on which scholarly&nbsp;
paper you are thinking about. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.621 --> 00:03:18.681
a couple hundred episodes ago, we talked&nbsp;
about an absolutely amazing paper that allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.681 --> 00:03:26.661
brain-to-text transcription. It took patients&nbsp;
who had a paralyzed hand and all the test subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.661 --> 00:03:34.341
has to do is imagine writing the letters, and&nbsp;
they magically appear on the screen. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.341 --> 00:03:40.521
hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars, and just&nbsp;
look at how quickly it goes! 90 characters per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.521 --> 00:03:49.401
minute with over 94% of accuracy, which can&nbsp;
be improved to over 99% of accuracy with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.401 --> 00:03:55.821
additional autocorrect technique. That is&nbsp;
absolutely amazing. We can learn a ton from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.821 --> 00:04:01.341
these papers. For instance, in that one, we&nbsp;
learned that it is quite remarkable that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:08.481
years after paralysis, the motor cortex is still&nbsp;
strong enough to be read by a brain-computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:16.041
interface well enough for such typing speed and&nbsp;
accuracy. It truly feels like we are living in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.041 --> 00:04:21.021
science fiction world. Imagine how many people&nbsp;
around the world we could help with this!

00:04:21.021 --> 00:04:29.781
So today, brain to text to possible, and brain&nbsp;
to audio is also possible. But here comes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.781 --> 00:04:34.461
best part - for people who are unable&nbsp;
to communicate with us through speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:42.561
they can not only write to us, but with this,&nbsp;
we can even reconstruct the musicality of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.561 --> 00:04:48.081
speech as well straight from the brain.&nbsp;
Imagine if we could have heard the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.081 --> 00:04:53.181
Stephen Hawking talk like that. How&nbsp;
amazing would that have been? Wow.

00:05:51.801 --> 00:05:56.061
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

